1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:07,633
The brush uncovers plaster, and on the plaster, paint — a border of flowers and bands, the colours still readable.

2
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:24,267
Almost no ancient Indian painting survives, because paint needs a surface that lasts and a place out of the weather.

3
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:26,615
Inside a cave cut into a cliff, it can.

4
00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:30,378
This episode is about the people who paid for those caves.

5
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:36,356
Inscriptions, including some inside the caves naming donors.

6
00:00:36,356 --> 00:00:39,275
Excavation and the surviving caves themselves.

7
00:00:39,275 --> 00:00:40,642
And texts, sparingly.

8
00:00:40,642 --> 00:00:47,836
The Vakatakas are not a dynasty with a great literature about them; they are known mostly from what they left.

9
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:56,623
The Vakatakas ruled much of the Deccan from about the middle of the third century, while the Guptas ruled the north.

10
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,986
They split into branches — the main one and the Vatsagulma branch — and they matter for the exam in two ways:

11
00:01:04,986 --> 00:01:08,443
their link to the Guptas, and their patronage of the caves.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:12,988
Here is the link.

13
00:01:12,988 --> 00:01:19,050
Chandragupta the Second married his daughter Prabhavatigupta to the Vakataka ruler Rudrasena the Second.

14
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:30,304
When Rudrasena died early, Prabhavatigupta governed as regent for her sons for some two decades — and her own inscriptions record her using her Gupta descent,

15
00:01:30,304 --> 00:01:32,420
which tells you how the alliance worked.

16
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:37,983
It gave the Guptas a friendly power on their southern flank while they fought the Shakas in the west.

17
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:53,297
And then Ajanta: a horseshoe cliff above a river, with caves cut into it over two widely separated periods.

18
00:01:53,297 --> 00:01:56,956
The earlier group belongs to the Satavahana centuries.

19
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:05,464
The later group — the larger and more famous one — was cut in the fifth century, in the Vakataka period.

20
00:02:05,464 --> 00:02:13,318
They are chaitya halls and viharas, carved out of solid rock from the top down, which means the sculptors could make no corrections.

21
00:02:15,933 --> 00:02:18,501
The paintings are what make Ajanta famous.

22
00:02:18,501 --> 00:02:24,474
Pigment on a prepared plaster ground, in a technique related to but not identical with true fresco.

23
00:02:24,474 --> 00:02:30,805
The subjects are largely Jataka stories — tales of the Buddha's previous lives — along with court scenes,

24
00:02:30,805 --> 00:02:33,553
animals, flowers and elaborate ceilings.

25
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:41,713
Following this series' rule, we are showing the ornament and the borders rather than the figures.

26
00:02:41,713 --> 00:02:49,007
What you should carry away is that this is the best surviving body of ancient Indian painting, and nearly all of it is in caves.

27
00:02:53,767 --> 00:02:54,346
Who paid?

28
00:02:54,346 --> 00:02:58,518
One influential reconstruction compresses the later caves into a short,

29
00:02:58,518 --> 00:03:05,017
intense phase under the Vakataka ruler Harishena and his ministers, essentially a single campaign of work.

30
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:14,997
Another reads the donor inscriptions inside the caves as evidence of many separate patrons over a longer stretch.

31
00:03:14,997 --> 00:03:17,690
The dating of individual caves is still argued.

32
00:03:17,690 --> 00:03:21,113
What is not in dispute is that this is Vakataka-period work,

33
00:03:21,113 --> 00:03:25,790
and that monks, officials and merchants as well as kings are named as donors.

34
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:33,172
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:33,172 --> 00:03:36,708
For Ajanta, remember: painted, not carved.

36
00:03:36,708 --> 00:03:40,133
Ellora, next unit, is the carved one.

37
00:03:45,367 --> 00:03:46,073
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:46,073 --> 00:03:47,416
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:47,416 --> 00:03:53,915
First: the Gupta princess who married a Vakataka ruler and governed as regent for her sons.

40
00:03:53,915 --> 00:03:55,416
Prabhavatigupta.

41
00:03:58,933 --> 00:04:05,446
Second: the horseshoe cliff above a river where caves were cut in two widely separated periods.

42
00:04:05,446 --> 00:04:06,289
Ajanta.

43
00:04:08,833 --> 00:04:15,352
Third: the category of stories, about the Buddha's previous lives, that the paintings mostly depict.

44
00:04:15,352 --> 00:04:16,492
The Jatakas.

45
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:24,748
In Mains, a question that comes up is what the Deccan caves tell us about patronage.

46
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:30,336
Use the donor inscriptions: kings, yes, but also ministers, monks and merchants.

47
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:40,186
A painted panel takes its place in the gallery, one of the few pieces of ancient Indian painting anybody can still look at.

48
00:04:44,267 --> 00:04:52,142
Next gallery: two southern dynasties who fought each other for centuries and, between them, worked out how to build a stone temple.

49
00:04:52,142 --> 00:04:55,843
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

